TOPIC:
Anatomy and Inference of Large Language Models

EXAMPLES:
['Llama 2 70B: A model with 70 billion parameters, requiring 140GB of storage '
 'space (at 2 bytes per parameter), trained on roughly 10 terabytes of text '
 'data.',
 "Next-word prediction: Given the sequence 'Cat sat on a', the model might "
 "predict 'mat' with a 97% probability based on its learned parameters."]

INTUITION:
["Think of an LLM as a very sophisticated 'autocomplete' machine. It doesn't "
 "'know' facts in the way humans do; it has simply seen so many patterns of "
 'text that it can predict what words usually follow others with high '
 'accuracy.',
 'Training a model is like creating a massive, lossy ZIP file of the internet. '
 "It doesn't hold the original documents perfectly, but it holds a compressed, "
 'summarized version of the knowledge within them.',
 "When an LLM 'hallucinates' (like creating a fake ISBN number), it is simply "
 'following the statistical pattern it learned: it knows that an ISBN label is '
 'typically followed by a long string of numbers, so it makes up a string of '
 "numbers that 'looks' correct."]

REASONING:
['Next-word prediction is a powerful training objective because it requires '
 'the model to develop an internal understanding of the world, entities, and '
 'facts to accurately predict text in diverse contexts.',
 'Neural networks can be considered a form of lossy compression because they '
 "do not store the training data verbatim; instead, they capture the 'gestalt' "
 'or underlying patterns and distribution of the information.',
 'The reason for using 2 bytes per parameter (float16) is to balance precision '
 'with memory efficiency, allowing massive models to be stored in manageable '
 'file sizes (e.g., 140GB for a 70B model).']

WHY IT MATTERS:
['Understanding that LLMs are next-token predictors explains why they '
 "occasionally 'dream' or hallucinate information—they are mimicking the "
 'distribution of their training data rather than accessing a verified '
 'database.']
